          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A mother of a 2-year-old with confirmed global developmental delay asks when the</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A mother of a 2-year-old with confirmed global developmental delay asks when therapy should begin. Investigations are still pending. What is the most appropriate advice?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Wait until all investigations are complete before starting any intervention</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Investigations and therapy proceed in parallel. Delaying therapy until a definitive aetiological diagnosis is reached wastes the critical window of neuroplasticity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Begin multi-domain early intervention now, during the period of maximal neuroplasticity</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Evidence consistently shows that early, intensive, multi-domain therapy during the first 2–3 years of life — the period of greatest neuroplasticity — yields substantially better functional and cognitive outcomes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Start physiotherapy alone and add other therapies only if there is no improvement after 6 months</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Management of developmental delay is multi-disciplinary from the outset. Speech, occupational, and other therapies should begin concurrently as clinically indicated, not sequentially.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Defer therapy until age 5 when a definitive IQ-based diagnosis can guide the plan</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Waiting until age 5 for formal IQ testing misses the critical early-intervention window. Therapy should begin as soon as delay is identified, well before a formal intellectual disability diagnosis can be made.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Early intervention — beginning within the first 2–3 years of life during peak neuroplasticity — yields the best outcomes. Therapy should not wait for completed investigations or a formal IQ diagnosis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Early, multi-domain intervention during peak neuroplasticity is one of the most evidence-backed principles in developmental paediatrics."
                }
              ],
